  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The other condition gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Their intersection is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For C, collect the terms on one side without multiplying by an unknown sign: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The zeros 0 and 4 and the pole 1 split the line into four intervals. The fraction has signs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></math> from left to right, so its positive set is precisely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Answer C.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> A gives only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; B includes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> instead; D has positive set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; E gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; F omits the upper restriction. Thus no other listed set agrees.</p>"
